{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0109","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"How many Blogger users and how many words does the Stateology dataset cover?","answer":"It covers close to 200,000 Blogger users in the U.S. and a dataset of over two billion words."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0109","fact_refs":[2],"question":"During what time period did the researchers collect the blogger profile data for this study?","answer":"They collected the data during May-July 2015."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0109","fact_refs":[3,4,5,6],"question":"How many bloggers and blogs were identified, and how many posts were downloaded per blog?","answer":"197,527 bloggers with state location information were identified, owning a total of 335,698 blogs. The 21 most recent postings were downloaded from each blog, yielding 4,600,465 blog posts total."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0109","fact_refs":[7,8,9,10],"question":"Which U.S. states have the most and fewest bloggers in the dataset?","answer":"California is densest with 11,701 users, followed by Texas (9,252) and New York (9,136). Delaware has the fewest bloggers, with 1,217 users."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0109","fact_refs":[13],"question":"What are the top five blogger-dense cities identified in the study?","answer":"In order: Chicago, New York, Portland, Seattle, and Atlanta."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0109","fact_refs":[14],"question":"What is the gender breakdown among bloggers who self-reported their gender?","answer":"Out of 153,209 users who self-reported gender, 52,725 were men and 100,484 were women."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0109","fact_refs":[11],"question":"How strongly does blogger density correlate with a state's population?","answer":"It correlates strongly, with a Spearman's rank correlation of 0.91 and a p-value below 0.0001."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0110","statement":"The paper proposes a domain adaptation approach for NMT using large out-of-domain training data plus a small amount of in-domain training data.","source_span":"the availability of large out-of-domain training data and a small in-domain training data"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0110","statement":"The proposed adaptation method can adapt an already-trained NMT system to a new domain within a few hours.","source_span":"fast enough to adapt an already trained system to a new domain within few hours"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0110","statement":"Retraining an NMT model on combined in-domain and out-of-domain data usually takes several days or weeks depending on data volume.","source_span":"which usually takes several days/weeks depending on the volume of the data"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0110","statement":"The authors report significant gains from their method on two language pairs, using both automatic metrics and human subjective evaluation.","source_span":"significant gains with our proposed method in both automatic metrics and a human subjective evaluation metric on two language pairs"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0110","statement":"The method decodes using an ensemble of the baseline model and the adapted (further-trained) model.","source_span":"we decode using an ensemble of the baseline model and the adapted model"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0110","statement":"BIBREF5's approach of further training an existing NMT system on in-domain data only reported an absolute gain of 3.8 BLEU points over the original model without further training.","source_span":"The authors report an absolute gain of 3.8 Bleu points compared to using an original model without further training."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0110","statement":"The paper's in-house NMT implementation is an attention-based encoder-decoder network whose encoder uses a bi-directional RNN.","source_span":"The encoder employs a bi-directional RNN to encode the source sentence"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0110","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is the core idea behind the domain adaptation approach proposed for Neural Machine Translation in this paper?","answer":"It leverages an already-available large out-of-domain training dataset together with a small amount of in-domain training data to adapt an NMT system to a new domain."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0110","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"How long does the proposed NMT adaptation method take compared to retraining from scratch on combined data?","answer":"The proposed method adapts an already-trained system to a new domain within a few hours, whereas retraining on combined data usually takes several days or weeks."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0110","fact_refs":[3],"question":"On how many language pairs did the authors evaluate their domain adaptation method, and how?","answer":"They evaluated it on two language pairs, using both automatic metrics and a human subjective evaluation metric."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0110","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How does the proposed method decode translations to avoid overfitting on in-domain data?","answer":"It decodes using an ensemble of the original baseline model and the further-trained (adapted) model."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0110","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What BLEU point improvement did the prior approach (BIBREF5) achieve by further training an NMT system on in-domain data only?","answer":"It achieved an absolute gain of 3.8 BLEU points compared to the original model without further training."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0110","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What type of encoder is used in the paper's attention-based NMT implementation?","answer":"An encoder-decoder network whose encoder uses a bi-directional RNN to encode the source sentence."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0111","statement":"The paper uses layer-wise relevance propagation (LRP) to trace text classification decisions back to individual words.","source_span":"tracing the classification decision back to individual words using layer-wise relevance propagation (LRP)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0111","statement":"Two word-based ML models are trained: a convolutional neural network (CNN) and a bag-of-words SVM classifier.","source_span":"We train two word-based ML models, a convolutional neural network (CNN) and a bag-of-words SVM classifier"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0111","statement":"The models are trained and evaluated on a topic categorization task of newsgroup documents.","source_span":"topic categorization of newsgroup documents"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0111","statement":"LRP produces relevance scores indicating how much individual words contribute to the overall classification decision.","source_span":"Resulting scores indicate how much individual words contribute to the overall classification decision."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0111","statement":"The paper introduces a novel way of generating vector-based document representations from word-wise relevance scores that capture semantic information.","source_span":"we introduce a novel way of generating vector-based document representations which capture semantic information"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0111","statement":"The paper introduces a measure of model explanatory power and shows the CNN has higher explainability than the SVM despite similar classification accuracy.","source_span":"the latter exhibits a higher level of explainability which makes it more comprehensible for humans"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0111","statement":"The paper shows quantitatively that LRP better identifies relevant words in text than sensitivity analysis.","source_span":"It is also shown quantitatively that LRP better identifies relevant words than sensitivity analysis."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0111","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What technique does this paper use to explain which words drive a text classifier's prediction?","answer":"Layer-wise relevance propagation (LRP), a technique that decomposes classifier predictions back onto individual words."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0111","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"Which two machine learning models are compared in the paper, and on what task?","answer":"A convolutional neural network (CNN) and a bag-of-words SVM classifier, compared on a topic categorization task of newsgroup documents."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0111","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What do the relevance scores produced by LRP indicate?","answer":"They indicate how much individual words contribute to the overall text classification decision."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0111","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What novel document representation does the paper introduce, and what does it capture?","answer":"A novel vector-based document representation built from word-wise LRP relevance scores, which captures semantic information."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0111","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How do the CNN and SVM models compare in classification accuracy versus explainability?","answer":"They achieve similar classification accuracy, but the CNN exhibits a higher level of explainability (explanatory power) than the SVM."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0111","fact_refs":[6],"question":"How does LRP compare to sensitivity analysis for identifying relevant words in a document?","answer":"The paper shows quantitatively that LRP better identifies relevant words than sensitivity analysis."}
